libc for bcc09

This will become the port of the dev86 C library.  So far it has:

  crt0.s    start-up code (see below)
  bcc/      run-time helpers that the compiler calls for int and long
            operations the 6809 can't do in a few instructions
  stdlib/   exit
  string/   memcpy (which the compiler also calls to copy structures)

"make" builds crt0.o and libc.a with this tree's as09 and ar09; "make
install" puts them in $(PREFIX)/lib/bcc09, where bcc09 tells ld09 to
look for them (bcc09 links with -C0, which is crt0.o, and -lc).  Each routine that a program may need on its
own is in its own module, since ld09 only loads the members of a
library that something refers to.

Start-up code
-------------

crt0.o is for the default target (bcc09 -Ms): a program loaded and
called by something it can return to, such as Color BASIC's EXEC, a
monitor or a debugger.  It runs on the stack its loader gave it: start
(the program's entry point) clears the BSS, saves S and calls main.
When main returns, or the program calls exit() or _exit(), the status
goes in D, S is restored, and an RTS returns to the loader.  Programs
that have no loader to return to, such as ROMs, will need start-up code
of their own.  There is nothing in exit() yet to flush stdio or call
atexit() functions, since libc doesn't have them.

bcc09 links programs at address 0 unless told otherwise, so give it a
load address for ld09, for example: bcc09 -o prog -X-T0x3000 prog.c

Calling conventions
-------------------

The run-time helpers for 16-bit int and unsigned operations (cc1's
softop.c) take the left operand in D and the right one in X, and leave
the result in D.  The name ends in "u" for unsigned operands.  They may
change D, X and CC, and must leave every other register as it was:
the compiler keeps values in Y and U across the call.

  imul, imulu     D * X (the low 16 bits are the same either way)
  idiv, idivu     D / X
  imod, imodu     D % X
  isl, islu       D << X
  isr             D >> X, arithmetic
  isru            D >> X, logical

The helpers for 32-bit long and unsigned long operations (cc1's
longop.c and hardop.c) hold a long in the register pair D:X, with the
high word in D and the low word in X (longs are big-endian, like the
6809).  For a binary operation, the left operand is in D:X and Y points
at the right one, high word first; the result is in D:X.  A shift
count, an int, is in Y instead.  The name ends in "u" for unsigned
operands (where it matters), then "_b" for big-endian.  They may change
D, X, Y and CC, and must leave U as it was.

  ladd_b, lsub_b                  D:X + [Y], D:X - [Y]
  land_b, lor_b, leor_b           D:X & [Y], | [Y], ^ [Y]
  lmul_b                          D:X * [Y] (low 32 bits)
  ldiv_b, ldivu_b                 D:X / [Y]
  lmod_b, lmodu_b                 D:X % [Y]
  lsl_b, lsr_b, lsru_b            D:X << Y, >> Y (arithmetic, logical)
  lneg_b, lcom_b                  -D:X, ~D:X
  lcmp_b, lcmpu_b                 set CC as a 32-bit compare of D:X
                                  with [Y] would, for signed (LT, GE,
                                  ...) or unsigned (LO, HS, ...) branches
  ltst_b                          set CC as comparing D:X with 0 would
  linc_b, ldec_b                  add 1 to, or subtract 1 from, the long
                                  at X; change only it and CC (the
                                  compiler keeps the old value in D:U)

(laddu_b, lsubu_b, landu_b, loru_b, leoru_b, lmulu_b and lslu_b are the
same routines as their signed names.)

Ordinary C functions such as memcpy take their first argument in X and
the others on the stack, in order, above the return address; the caller
removes them.  An int or pointer result is returned in X.

Behavior C leaves undefined: signed division truncates toward zero and
the remainder has the sign of the dividend (as C99 requires); division
by zero doesn't trap (see bcc/idiv.s and bcc/ldiv.s); shift counts of
the operand's size or more (16 or 32), taken as unsigned, give 0, or
copies of the sign bit for a signed right shift.

The tests in tests/ (ops.c, longops.c, regtest.s, and crtret.c and
crtexit.c for crt0 and exit) check all of this on sim6809.
